  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM Tuesday...\n\n1) Reduced svr tstm threat the rest of today, with localized flash \nflood threat still a possibility. \n\nA cold front just north of the Virginia border will move south \nacross central North Carolina tonight. Ahead of the front, the \natmosphere will remain very moist and moderately unstable, with \nlatest mesoanalysis showing PWATs around 1.6 to 2.0 inches and \nMLCAPE generally around 500-1500 J/kg (highest MLCAPE southern half \nof our CWA). However, wind shear will remain very weak, limiting \nstorm organization and favoring slow-moving storms. While a few \nstrong to marginally severe storms (damaging winds gusts the \nconcern) will still be possible across the far SW portion of our \nCWA, the greater concern will be locally heavy rainfall and isolated \nflooding, as slow storm motions could allow storms to produce very \nheavy downpours or repeatedly move over the same areas. Storm \ncoverage is still expected to increase through the rest of this \nafternoon as the front approaches and various triggering mechanisms \ncurrently exist, such as outflows, localized convergent flow, and \ndifferential heating boundaries. Once we see coverage expand, we \nexpect scattered thunderstorms to continue into the evening. The \nexact location of the heaviest convection remains uncertain, but \nareas across northern and central NC could see locally higher \nrainfall totals where storms train. Given recent dry conditions, \nwidespread flooding is not expected, though localized flooding could \noccur, particularly in urban and poor-drainage areas.\n\nThunderstorms should gradually weaken during the evening as \ninstability decreases; however showers may become widespread \novernight as the front moves through. Rain will likely continue into \nthe early morning hours Wednesday, especially across the Piedmont \nand western portions of central NC. Through Wednesday morning, most \nlocations should receive around 0.5 to 1.0 inch of rain, with \nlocally higher amounts of 1 to 2 inches or more possible where \nthunderstorms repeatedly develop or move over the same area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cold air damming will bring much below normal \ntemperatures during mid to late week. \n\nThe leading edge of the surface cold front, currently draped from \nnear Richmond southwest to near the VA/NC border and back northwest \nto just southwest of Blacksburg, VA. This front has moved little \nover the past 24 hours since the strong area of high pressure \nremains elongated west-east as it slides into southern Quebec. This \nis expected to change tonight as upper divergence overspreads a \nbroad area of low pressure off the Carolina coast, and as it \ndeepens, strengthening north-northeastly flow will pull the cold \nfront, later to be deemed the CAD front, equatorward and into the \nCarolinas by Wed morning. Moist isentropic ascent in the 295-305K \nlayer will provide diabatic support to lock in the CAD, especially \nalong and north of the favored I-85 corridor where persistent low \nclouds and drizzle is likely through Wed night. Periods of moderate \nrain appears likely Wed evening into the overnight period as a \nconsolidated shortwave trough ejects across the southern \nAppalachians before closing off over the VA/NC Piedmont by Thurs \nmorning.\n\nCAD erosion is always difficult to forecast, but a favorable setup \nfor dispersion is expected Thurs into Fri with an area of low \npressure deepening over the western Atlantic as it shifts northward \nwith time. Although local research suggests CAD dispersion should \nbegin once the low reaches the latitude of the VA/NC, which should \noccur by Wed night, the reinforcing shortwave closing over the \nwestern Piedmont should continue to provide lift and support low \novercast and drizzle throughout the day on Thurs. However, as the \narea of low pressure continues to deepen Thurs afternoon, another \nsurge of slightly cooler but significantly drier air should erode \nthe classic CAD pattern from NE to SW Thurs night into Fri. Below \nnormal temperatures will persist into the weekend as continued \nperturbations rotating around the backside of the closed low just \noff the Northeast coast will support persistent northerly flow over \nthe Mid-Atlantic, although likely gradually moderating with time as \nlow-lvl thicknesses steadily rise.",
